1.1.text:
Article 23

In addition to establishing PPT in the Protection of Women and Children as referred to in Article 22, in order to support the implementation of the provision of protection to women and children from acts of violence, the following are established:
a. Task force on the crime of trafficking in persons;
b. Regional action committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
c. Task force for the protection of women and children at the sub-district level;
d. Domestic violence prevention groups at the village/sub-district level; and
e. Other forms of tasks that support the Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Women and Children.

Article 4

The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence and discrimination are:
a. to prevent acts of violence and discrimination against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. to eliminate all forms of violence, discrimination, and exploitation against women and children;
c. to protect women and children from acts of violence and discrimination in obtaining their constitutional rights;
d. to protect and provide security for women and children;
e. to provide services and protection to women and children victims of acts of violence and discrimination, reporters, and witnesses; and
f. to facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.

Article 9

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 8 letter a, include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent shall establish programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan as a basis for OPD and
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be set forth in the strategic plan of OPD.
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 12

Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence against women and children is obliged to make efforts in accordance with their capacity to:
a. prevent and stop the continuation of acts of violence;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. assist in the process of submitting an application for protection order.

CHAPTER VII

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 13

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance, and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows:
a. increasing the number and quality of educational units both formal and non-formal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement, and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

CHAPTER II

SCOPE

Article 2

The scope of protection of women and children from acts of violence, is as follows:
a. principles and objectives;
b. forms of violence;
c. rights of women and children victims of violence;
d. obligations and responsibilities;
e. prevention of acts of violence;
f. special protection of children;
g. services and empowerment of victims of acts of violence;
h. institutions;
i. cooperation and partnerships;
j. reporting;
k. guidance and supervision; and
l. financing.

REGIONAL REGULATION OF BELITUNG REGENCY

NUMBER 2 OF 2018

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN'S AND CHILDREN'S RIGHTS FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF BELITUNG,

Considering:

a. that discrimination and violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights and crimes against humanity, and are inconsistent with Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that in order to fulfill the constitutional rights of women and children and to improve the quality of life of women and children is one of the mandatory affairs that is the duty, authority, and responsibility of the Regional Government;
c. that violence against women and children in Belitung Regency tends to increase and spread, thus requiring integrated protection efforts;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation of Belitung Regency concerning the Protection of Women's and Children's Rights from Acts of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions and Municipalities in South Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);
3. Law Number 27 of 2000 concerning the Establishment of Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 217, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4033);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
7. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence, (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
8. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 446);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BELITUNG REGENCY

and

REGENT OF BELITUNG

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN'S AND CHILDREN'S RIGHTS FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 17

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. social rehabilitation and reintegration;
e. repatriation;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms in police lines;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
3. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by related Regional Apparatus Organizations whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
4. In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with government agencies, other regional governments, non-governmental organizations, and social institutions.
5. Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling services for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 14

1. Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 13, shall be implemented….

D:\PERDA PDF\2-PERDA PERLINDUNGAN HAK PEREMPUAN DAN ANAK.doc 10 implemented by OPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. youth and sports;
g. population and civil registration;
h. mental and spiritual; and
i. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by OPD and UKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.
3. In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions;
d. health institutions;
e. social community institutions; and
f. business world and other institutions.

CHAPTER IV

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

Forms of violence against women and children include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. coercion/deprivation of liberty;
f. exploitation;
g. threats of certain actions; and/or
h. other violence.

Article 28

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with:
a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. other Regency/City Governments; and
d. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. provision of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses;
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER III

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 3

Protection of women and children from violence and discrimination is organized based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. victim protection;
e. empowerment; and
f. integration.

CHAPTER VIII

SPECIAL PROTECTION OF CHILDREN

Article 15

1. Special protection of children is provided to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. child victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorism networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

